  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Establishment of a wet lab for the adaptive management of coral reef restoration in the Southeastern Dominican Republic through a trained body of community technicians and volunteers</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to support a conservation effort in Southeastern Reefs Marine Sanctuary (SMASE) in the Dominican Republic by reducing threats to coral reefs and enhancing their resilience to adapt to their changing environment. Coral reefs, along with mangroves and seagrass beds, are key ecosystems for the conservation of marine flora and fauna. This project is intended to conserve the coral reef ecosystem by addressing both natural and anthropogenic threats such as pollution, sedimentation, coastal development, overfishing, sea level rise, thermal stress, and lack of community capacity to enforce fishing and tourism regulations. Proposed project activities include: 1) The design and implementation of an adaptive management protocol, which will expand and increase the efficacy of coral conservation/restoration efforts; 2) Establishment of a Wet Lab for coral asexual and sexual reproduction restoration strategies of key reef species, such as Acropora Cervicornis, A. palmata, Diploria laberynthisformis, and Orbicella faveolata; 3) Strengthened relations with public-private partners for effective reef management, by training a body of volunteers as lab technicians and tour guides; and 4) Increasing the reach of the project by sharing and replicating results at the local, national and international level.</narrative>
  </description>
